WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.220
At rest, each side of an adult human heart pumps about 5 litres of blood a minute.

2
00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:12.420
Over any few minutes, it cannot pump out more than flows back in.

3
00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:14.620
So what sets cardiac output?

4
00:00:15.220 --> 00:00:17.200
The heart, or the veins that fill it?

5
00:00:17.883 --> 00:00:20.903
The Fick principle is bookkeeping for oxygen.

6
00:00:21.343 --> 00:00:25.583
At steady state, the oxygen the lungs take up each minute leaves in the blood

7
00:00:25.583 --> 00:00:26.603
flowing through them.

8
00:00:26.943 --> 00:00:29.183
Take made-up numbers for an adult at rest.

9
00:00:29.743 --> 00:00:32.963
Uptake is 260 millilitres of O2 per min.

10
00:00:33.563 --> 00:00:36.623
Arterial blood holds 195 millilitres per litre.

11
00:00:37.503 --> 00:00:40.423
Mixed venous blood from the pulmonary artery holds 143.

12
00:00:41.083 --> 00:00:42.243
The difference is 52.

13
00:00:42.243 --> 00:00:46.323
260 divided by 52 is 5 litres per min.

14
00:00:47.083 --> 00:00:51.303
Stop the heart, and the systemic circulation settles at one pressure.

15
00:00:51.863 --> 00:00:55.463
That zero flow pressure is the mean systemic filling pressure.

16
00:00:55.983 --> 00:01:01.163
In a tank, fluid below the outlet rim, the unstressed volume, fills it without

17
00:01:01.163 --> 00:01:02.303
stretching the walls.

18
00:01:03.103 --> 00:01:07.463
Fluid above the rim, the stressed volume, stretches them and pushes back.

19
00:01:08.143 --> 00:01:09.623
Adding blood raises the pressure.

20
00:01:10.263 --> 00:01:14.783
Guyton's group measured about six to seven millimetres of mercury in anaesthetized

21
00:01:14.783 --> 00:01:18.243
dogs, a dog value, not a normal human one.

22
00:01:19.033 --> 00:01:23.633
Guyton's group replaced the right heart with an external pump, in open-chest dogs.

23
00:01:24.273 --> 00:01:27.073
They set right atrial pressure and measured the return.

24
00:01:27.733 --> 00:01:31.433
As they lowered the pressure, return rose along a roughly straight line.

25
00:01:31.933 --> 00:01:37.273
It stopped rising at about minus-2 to minus-4 millimetres of mercury because veins

26
00:01:37.273 --> 00:01:42.433
collapse. Return reached zero where atrial pressure equals filling pressure.

27
00:01:42.933 --> 00:01:44.993
That line is the venous return curve.

28
00:01:44.993 --> 00:01:50.313
Flow equals filling pressure minus atrial pressure, divided by the resistance to

29
00:01:50.313 --> 00:01:51.313
venous return.

30
00:01:51.967 --> 00:01:53.427
The heart has a curve too.

31
00:01:54.207 --> 00:01:57.907
Higher atrial pressure fills the ventricle more, so output rises.

32
00:01:58.687 --> 00:02:03.847
Averaged over time, output equals venous return, so the circulation settles where

33
00:02:03.847 --> 00:02:08.327
the lines cross. This is an illustrative model, not fitted to data.

34
00:02:08.927 --> 00:02:12.647
Three plus atrial pressure equals seven minus atrial pressure.

35
00:02:13.107 --> 00:02:17.967
That gives atrial pressure two millimetres of mercury output five litres per min.

36
00:02:18.750 --> 00:02:21.330
In the model, move one line at a time.

37
00:02:21.910 --> 00:02:23.870
A stronger heart lifts its line.

38
00:02:24.810 --> 00:02:28.090
Output rises to six and atrial pressure falls to one.

39
00:02:28.550 --> 00:02:29.950
A weaker heart drops it.

40
00:02:30.350 --> 00:02:34.010
Output falls to four and atrial pressure rises to three.

41
00:02:34.670 --> 00:02:39.710
Over hours to days, venoconstriction and retained salt and water raise filling

42
00:02:39.710 --> 00:02:42.810
pressure to nine and the veins' line slides right.

43
00:02:43.390 --> 00:02:46.630
Output is back to five, atrial pressure at four.

44
00:02:47.080 --> 00:02:51.550
The rule to keep: output sits where the heart's line crosses the veins' line.
